    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The corona of a black hole is a region that consists of highly energized particles above the orbiting matter of the accretion disk. X-rays are emitted from the corona and either travel directly to us or illuminate the accretion disk. This illumination of the inner disk is enhanced by gravitational light bending, which focuses the rays towards the black hole and therefore towards the inner radii of the disk. These rays that hit the inner radii are reflected back to us, and we observe them in the X-ray reflection spectrum. This reflection has proved to be a powerful tool for understanding a black hole’s properties and the environment just outside its event horizon. In this work, we create novel general relativistic ray tracing simulations to investigate the effects of altering the geometry of the accretion disks of black holes on their reflection spectra. Previous analyses have assumed a simplistic system, consisting of a lamppost corona with a flat and infinitesimally thin accretion disk. We extend these models to more realistic accretion disk approximations. These include the radiation-pressure-dominated Shakura-Sunyaev disk, an elliptical disk that has a non-negligible scale height in its inner regions due to radiation pressure, a wedged disk that has a constant aspect ratio, as well as various \"warped\" disks. In the accretion geometries with non-negligible thickness, we observed an increased number of strongly redshifted photons (∼ 2.5–5 keV) and an associated reduction in the relative number of moderately redshifted and blueshifted photons. The behavior of the spectra from warped disk geometries are dependent on the azimuthal angle of the observation. We show that all of these different geometries with certain parameters for the observation and geometry of the disk can be distinguished with the next generation of high-resolution spectroscopy missions, including the X-Ray Imaging and Spectroscopy Mission (XRISM) and Athena.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
